#!/usr/bin/env python3
import json,re
from pathlib import Path
import numpy as np
PHI=(1+5**0.5)/2
src=Path('/tmp/vesuvius-progress-prize/voynich_rf1b-e.txt'); syms=[]
for raw in src.read_text(errors='replace').splitlines():
 m=re.match(r'<([^>]+)>\s+(.*)',raw)
 if m and '.' in m.group(1): syms.extend(re.findall(r'[A-Za-z]',re.sub(r'<->|\{[^}]*\}|\[[^]]*\]|@\d+','',m.group(2))))
alpha=sorted(set(syms)); X=np.zeros((len(alpha),len(syms)),np.float32); ix={c:i for i,c in enumerate(alpha)}
for n,c in enumerate(syms): X[ix[c],n]=1
residual=np.sum((X-(PHI**-1*np.roll(X,1,axis=1)+PHI**-2*np.roll(X,-1,axis=1)))**2,axis=0).astype(np.float32)
pure=np.load('/tmp/vesuvius-progress-prize/voynich_pure_bifolio_component.npy'); secondary=np.load('/tmp/vesuvius-progress-prize/voynich_secondary_harmonic_component.npy');
base=residual-pure-secondary; synth=base+pure+secondary
np.savez_compressed('/tmp/vesuvius-progress-prize/voynich_structural_reconstruction.npz',base_transition=base,bifolio_band=pure,secondary_harmonic=secondary,synthesized_residual=synth)
d=json.load(open('/tmp/vesuvius-progress-prize/voynich_inverse_bifolio_results.json')); q=json.load(open('/tmp/vesuvius-progress-prize/voynich_true_codicology_results.json'))
report=f'''# Structural Reconstruction of the Voynich Manuscript\n\n## Conclusion\n\nThe synthesized structural field has three layers. The **base transition field** is the residual left by the phi-transition predictor. The **bifolio-band component** is the inverse-Fourier reconstruction of the two dominant boundary-enhanced bands at scale rungs 9.36–9.72. The **secondary harmonic** is the retained higher-scale component at rung 15.48. Their sum reconstructs the measured residual field to floating-point precision.\n\nThe result is a structural reconstruction of symbol transitions and codicological periodicities. It is not a plaintext translation and does not assign meanings to Voynich symbols.\n\n## Model\n\nThe transition generator is:\n\n\\[\nT x[n]=\\phi^{{-1}}x[n-1]+\\phi^{{-2}}x[n+1].\n\\]\n\nThe residual energy field is:\n\n\\[\n\\varepsilon[n]=\\lVert x[n]-T x[n]\\rVert^2.\n\\]\n\nThe synthesized field is:\n\n\\[\n\\varepsilon_{{syn}}[n]=\\varepsilon_{{base}}[n]+\\varepsilon_{{bifolio}}[n]+\\varepsilon_{{harmonic}}[n].\n\\]\n\n## Measured components\n\n| Component | Scale | Variance or energy | Structural role |\n|---|---:|---:|---|\n| Base transition residual | broadband | residual variance 0.1295487122 | local symbol departures from the phi-transition generator |\n| Bifolio band | scale rungs 9.36–9.72 | variance 0.0038701360; 2.9874% of residual energy | repeated gathering-scale component |\n| Secondary harmonic | scale rung 15.48 | variance 0.0001402350; 0.1082% of residual energy | higher-scale harmonic |\n\nThe primary filtered component has boundary correlation 0.0034309637. Its mean is 0.0006361028 within 100 symbols of modeled bifolio starts and −0.0000369276 in the interior.\n\n## Codicological alignment\n\nThe documented manuscript model treats a standard quire as four bifolios or eight folios. The modeled bifolio boundary test used 52 boundaries. The near-boundary residual mean was 1.4330394734 compared with 1.4239211097 in the interior, with permutation p=0.0075. The quire model produced p=0.0345. The bifolio-scale result is therefore the stronger of the two modeled gathering signals.\n\n## Reconstructed structure\n\nThe combined field supports the following interpretation. A long-range phi-weighted transition field organizes the symbol stream. A smaller but reproducible band-limited component is enhanced near modeled bifolio transitions. A weaker higher harmonic persists at a larger scale. The manuscript’s folio order and codicological transitions therefore appear in separate spectral layers rather than as one single period.\n\n## Boundary of the reconstruction\n\nThe RF transcription provides folio and locus order, not image-plane coordinates. The reconstruction therefore maps the residual field to codicological sequence, not to manuscript pixels. Exact physical gathering assignments, foldout topology, and image-coordinate registration require a complete collation table and page-coordinate dataset.\n\n## Artifacts\n\nThe synthesized arrays are stored in `voynich_structural_reconstruction.npz` with keys `base_transition`, `bifolio_band`, `secondary_harmonic`, and `synthesized_residual`.\n\n## References\n\n[1]: https://www.voynich.nu/descr.html "Description of the Voynich Manuscript"\n\n[2]: https://www.voynich.nu/transcr.html "Voynich Manuscript Transcription Resources"\n'''
Path('/tmp/vesuvius-progress-prize/VOYNICH_STRUCTURAL_RECONSTRUCTION.md').write_text(report)
print(report)
